\ A native carries the dispatch id its entries were registered under, so a binding named after a
\ generic native cannot capture the native's entries.

\ A binding named `-` in the caller leaves arithmetic intact in an unrelated word further down
\ the call stack.
victim: ( -- x ) [ 100.0 40.0 - ] ;
caller: ( -- x ) [ -: [ drop drop 99.0 ] ; victim ] ;

\ The prelude computes with its own words under the shadow: the quotation is written where `-`
\ still means the native, and `#map` runs it under a frame that binds `-` to something else.
inner-sub: ( -- a ) [ { 1 2 3 } [ 1 - ] #map #collect ] ;
with-shadow: ( -- a ) [ -: [ drop drop 0 ] ; inner-sub ] ;

victim .
caller .
with-shadow .

\ The same shape holds for every other native that carries its own dispatch identity. Each victim
\ below reaches an entry the native's fallthrough would not reproduce, so a captured identity shows
\ up as a different value or an error rather than as the same answer by another route.
len-victim: ( -- n ) [ { 1 2 3 } #len ] ;
len-caller: ( -- n ) [ #len: [ drop 0 ] ; len-victim ] ;

\ `>string` on a symbol drops the trailing colon its fallthrough writer keeps.
string-victim: ( -- s ) [ foo: >string ] ;
string-caller: ( -- s ) [ >string: [ drop "shadowed" ] ; string-victim ] ;

get-victim: ( -- n ) [ H{ a: 1 } a: @get ] ;
get-caller: ( -- n ) [ @get: [ drop drop 0 ] ; get-victim ] ;

bitand-victim: ( -- n ) [ 12 10 bitand ] ;
bitand-caller: ( -- n ) [ bitand: [ drop drop 0 ] ; bitand-victim ] ;

len-victim .
len-caller .
string-victim .
string-caller .
get-victim .
get-caller .
bitand-victim .
bitand-caller .

\ A typed container registers its mutation arms when the type is defined, so the registration and
\ the call site have to agree on the native's identity rather than on what its name resolves to at
\ either moment.
vector(fixnum): M{ inner-type: vector element-type: fixnum define: [ define-parameterized-type ] } ;

push-victim: ( -- s ) [ V{ 1 2 } >vector(fixnum) 3 #push! type-name ] ;
push-caller: ( -- s ) [ #push!: [ drop ] ; push-victim ] ;

\ A typed arm that delegates back to the raw native has to reach it by identity too, or the caller's
\ binding answers in the raw native's place.
nth-mut-victim: ( -- n ) [ V{ 1 2 } >vector(fixnum) 0 99 #nth! unmake-vector(fixnum) 0 #nth ] ;
nth-mut-caller: ( -- n ) [ #nth!: [ drop drop drop 0 ] ; nth-mut-victim ] ;

push-victim .
push-caller .
nth-mut-victim .
nth-mut-caller .
